WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.168 size:81% position:41%
-Thank you, thank you.
I'm happy to be here.

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:03.136 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Thank you.

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Cheering ]
Uh...

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:08.008 size:81% position:41% line:92%
[ Cheering continues ]

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:11.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't like the movie
"Home Alone."

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:12.479 line:92%
I get why some people do, man.

00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:15.382
'Cause some people, "Home Alone"
is ridiculous comedy.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:17.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
They laugh at it
like it's nonsensical.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.553
"Ha ha ha! Who would ever leave
a child unattended?

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.655 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
"Ha ha ha ha ha!

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:25.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
Who would ever leave
an 8-year-old boy home alone?"

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:28.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
Uh, my dad.
He did it all the time.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.163 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:34.134
The first time I watched
"Home Alone," I was home alone.

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:35.635 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:38.104 line:92%
I was praying for my life.

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.906 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:43.309
Because of that, I had to be
raised with my grandparents,

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, like, the only thing
I regret

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:49.482
about being raised by old people
is every now and then I'll say

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
something an old woman
would say.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
Like, this is a conversation

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:55.989 size:84% position:42%
I had with my roommate.
He was in the kitchen.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:57.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
I walked in. I'm like,
"Hey, man, what you doing?"

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.192 line:92%
He was like, "Washing dishes."

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:01.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like, "Not with cold
water, you ain't."

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.896 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Like that's...

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.532 line:92%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:10.537
That's something that came out
of my mouth to another adult.

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:14.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
I grew up in the hood,
but I'm not a thug.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.910 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Not for lack of trying.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.477 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just that whenever I try
to sound gangster,

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
my vernacular
always discredits me.

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'll give you guys an example.
I was at a house party.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.419
These dudes were trying to start
a fight with me and my homies.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.755
And the most intimidating thing
I could think about saying

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
was this -- I walked up on 'em
all aggressive.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:32.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, "Hey, dawg,
it'd behoove you

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.361 line:92%
to get to steppin', brother."

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:36.429 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.132
"Come out here
with that rigma-roo." [ Laughs ]

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:40.200 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Applause ]

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, man. I got beat up
that night, rightfully so.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:44.704 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
You don't say "behoove"
in the hood.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
You can't be a thug like,
"Hey, dawg,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
bequeath me my pistol."
It doesn't work.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:53.646 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:54.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
Where I'm from,
there's different rules.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, where I'm from,
it's rude to correct somebody

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.384 line:92%
if you know what they meant.

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:00.453
We all know these petty people,
right?

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.422
You say, like, "Hey, man,
I'll see you tomorrow."

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:03.523 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You mean today?
It's after midnight."

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:08.328 size:84% position:42%
"Like, shut up and die.
You know what I meant."

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:09.696 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughs ]

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:10.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
This happened to me
the other day.

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.967 size:81% position:41%
I was at the duck pond
minding my business.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.201 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.170 line:92%
Yes, I go to the duck pond.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
I told y'all, I was raised
with my grandparents.

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:23.176
Going to the duck pond
is like going to the club

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.644 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
for old people.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.745 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:28.248
So I was at the duck pond
sitting under a willow tree

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:29.883 size:88% position:44% line:92%
playing chess by myself.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.484 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:32.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
This guy overheard
my conversation.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.355
He waited till I got off the
phone to jump in and correct me.

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:39.325
He was like, "Excuse me, sir,
I think you meant to say geese."

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.360 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I was like, "What?"

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was like, "Yeah, geese.
More than one goose --

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.330 line:92%
they're actually called geese."

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.765 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like,
"Oh, what did I call 'em?"

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:49.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was like,
"You called them goosesses."

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:52.038 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:56.309
I'm like, "Come on, man, you
know what I mean. Goosesses."

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:59.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was upset not only that
he corrected me.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.115 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was upset that,
like, he sounded offended,

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:07.320
like I had somehow misgendered
a goose, you know what I mean?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
He made me feel like a drunk
redneck at a duck pond

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.958 line:92%
screaming racial obscenities.

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:14.928
Like... [ Country accent ]
"Look at all these goosesses!

00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:17.931 size:78% position:39%
Hey, you! Yeah, you!
Go back to Gooseica!"

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:22.502
[ Normal voice ] Like that.
That's how he made me feel, man.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:25.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm not like that.
I don't like to offend people.

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:28.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
I try my best not to.
Sometimes we can't avoid it.

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:31.110
I was on a flight and this lady
had her baby with her.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
And during our conversation
I asked her --

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like, "Hey,
what's your daughter's name?"

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she got upset
because it was a little boy.

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:39.986
And I tried my best to apologize
to her,

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:41.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
but she wouldn't accept
my apology.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:44.357
She was like, "Well, it's
obvious he's a little boy.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's wearing blue.
Boys wear blue."

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:49.729
And the best thing about this
conversation is she had on

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:51.564 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
a blue cardigan.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:52.899 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:54.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I was like,
"Well, you know what?

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You're right, sir,
I apologize."

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.171 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.171 --> 00:03:58.905 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.574 align:start size:94% position:6%
"No, for real. Man to man,
dude to dude, my bad.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:06.346
I didn't mean... I didn't mean
to ruin this father/son moment."

00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:07.013 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:11.784
The name of that joke is
"Gender is a social construct."

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.419 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:17.357
I'm following my dreams.
I don't recommend it. Uh...

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:17.924 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's hard, man. I do comedy
full-time, which means

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:21.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have to get, like,
part-time jobs,

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.363 size:81% position:41%
like mainly at, like,
fast food restaurants.

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:26.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I hate working
at fast food restaurants

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:28.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I feel like a monster,
right?

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:31.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like I'm harming
the American public, right?

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have to lie every time
I serve food.

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:34.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have to tell people,
have a nice day,

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
knowing they're going
to have explosive diarrhea

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.478 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
immediately after.

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:39.512 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.414 size:81% position:41% line:92%
It's the devil's work.

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.349 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:44.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the only reason I can
justify working

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:47.587
at a fast food restaurant,
it's the easiest job to get.

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:50.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's so easy to get
a fast food job,

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.925
you could mess up your interview
by being too professional.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
I learned that the hard way.
I had a interview,

00:04:55.662 --> 00:04:58.798
and I handed the guy my résumé.
He got scared and jumped back,

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:00.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, "Whoa, hold on, man,
what's that?"

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
"I was like, "It's my résumé."

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:04.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was like,
"Oh, I thought you was

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.406 line:92%
serving me court papers, dawg."

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:07.373 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:08.841 line:92%
"I thought that was a subpoena."

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:13.146
I'm Kiry Shabazz. You all
have been great. thank you.

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:16.382 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:19.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I like your "Home Alone."
That was a great joke.

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:21.721 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.589 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Kiry Shabazz!

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:25.224 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

